states.39f2f8db8692:
[
 {
  "seq": 1,
  "speaker": "Jolene Abara, Head of Investor Relations",
  "text": "Good morning and thank you for joining Aurelian Airways' March-quarter 2026 earnings call. With me are our CEO Ewan Castellano, President Folasade Brennan and CFO Ruairi Tanaka-Holm. Today's discussion contains forward-looking statements that reflect our current views and are subject to risks and uncertainties; please refer to our SEC filings for the factors that could cause actual results to differ. We will also discuss non-GAAP measures, reconciled in our earnings release. After prepared remarks, we will take questions from analysts and then from members of the media."
 },
 {
  "seq": 2,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "Thank you, Jolene, and good morning. Before the numbers, I want to thank our 98,000 people for running the best March-quarter operation in our history: we completed 99.6% of scheduled flights and our on-time arrival rate was 84%, which put us first among large US carriers for the ninth straight month. Our people earned $41 million in shared-rewards payouts for those operational goals. We also opened our refreshed lounge in Denver and added two new routes from our Raleigh station."
 },
 {
  "seq": 3,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "Financially, March-quarter revenue was a record $13.6 billion, up 2% year over year, and we earned an operating margin of 6.2%. Premium revenue grew 7%, loyalty revenue grew 8%, and cargo was up 4%. Main-cabin revenue, however, declined 3%, and since early March we have seen a clear softening in domestic main-cabin bookings as consumer and corporate confidence weakened amid trade tensions. Corporate travel volumes have been flat, with tech and media accounts cutting back while banking and consulting held steady."
 },
 {
  "seq": 4,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "So let me address the outlook directly. In January we guided to full-year earnings per share of more than $7.00 and free cash flow of more than $4 billion. Given the lack of economic clarity, it is premature at this time to provide an updated full-year outlook, and we are withdrawing that guidance. That is not a statement about our franchise; our balance sheet is the strongest it has been in a decade, and we will update you on the full year when visibility improves. We are, however, giving you a June-quarter guide, because we can see bookings for the next ninety days with reasonable confidence."
 },
 {
  "seq": 5,
  "speaker": "Folasade Brennan, President",
  "text": "Thanks, Ewan. On the commercial side, our co-brand credit card partner remuneration came in at $1.9 billion, up 9%, and new card acquisitions were up 5%. Premium seats now make up 31% of our capacity. Transatlantic summer bookings remain solid, and we added a seasonal route to Porto. Transpacific is improving, led by Seoul and Manila. Latin America remains challenged by excess industry capacity to Mexican beach destinations, and we have trimmed frequencies there."
 },
 {
  "seq": 6,
  "speaker": "Folasade Brennan, President",
  "text": "Because of the main-cabin softening, we are taking action on capacity. We are reducing planned capacity growth for the second half of the year from about 4% to flat year over year, mostly by retiring older narrowbodies early and trimming off-peak domestic flying on Tuesdays and Wednesdays. We will keep flexing capacity month by month if demand changes."
 },
 {
  "seq": 7,
  "speaker": "Ruairi Tanaka-Holm, CFO",
  "text": "Thank you, Folasade. For the June quarter, we expect total revenue to be flat to up 2% year over year, an operating margin of 11% to 14%, and earnings per share of $1.70 to $2.30. We expect fuel prices of $2.30 to $2.50 per gallon. Non-fuel unit costs are expected to rise 3% to 4%, reflecting lower capacity growth and wage rates from our pilot contract. Our liquidity is $7.2 billion, and we repaid $900 million of debt in the quarter."
 },
 {
  "seq": 8,
  "speaker": "Ruairi Tanaka-Holm, CFO",
  "text": "On capital spending, we now expect about $5 billion for the year, roughly $500 million lower than planned, as we defer some non-aircraft projects. We continue to prioritize debt reduction, and we remain on track for our leverage target of 1.5 times. We are not changing our dividend. A few housekeeping items: our effective tax rate for the June quarter should be about 24%, depreciation will run slightly higher as we accelerate retirements of older narrowbodies, and we contributed $250 million to our pension plans in the March quarter, which completes our planned funding for the year. Finally, our maintenance, repair and overhaul business, which services other carriers, grew third-party revenue by 18% and remains on track to roughly double in size by 2028."
 },
 {
  "seq": 9,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "We will now begin the analyst question-and-answer session. Our first question comes from Marisol Etxeberria with Calloway Brothers."
 },
 {
  "seq": 10,
  "speaker": "Marisol Etxeberria, Calloway Brothers",
  "text": "Thanks. Ewan, you've withdrawn the full year but given the June quarter. Can you give us any sense of a floor for the full year, maybe a recession scenario?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 11,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "I'd rather not put a scenario number out there that becomes the number. What I'll say is that we have been profitable in every quarter except the pandemic, and our cost and capacity levers are much stronger than they were in prior downturns. When we have better visibility into the back half, we'll come back with a full-year view."
 },
 {
  "seq": 12,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "Our next question is from Desmond Achterberg with Hollis & Grant."
 },
 {
  "seq": 13,
  "speaker": "Desmond Achterberg, Hollis & Grant",
  "text": "On aircraft tariffs: you have European-built widebodies scheduled for delivery this year. Will you pay the tariff on those airplanes?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 14,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "We will not pay tariffs on aircraft deliveries. If tariffs apply when an airplane is ready, we will defer that delivery and work with the manufacturer on timing. We have flexibility in our fleet plan to do that without hurting the summer schedule. We are not cancelling any orders."
 },
 {
  "seq": 15,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "Next question, Wen-Hsuan Liao with Morrow Quay."
 },
 {
  "seq": 16,
  "speaker": "Wen-Hsuan Liao, Morrow Quay",
  "text": "Where exactly is demand weakest? Is it premium, corporate or the back of the plane?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 17,
  "speaker": "Folasade Brennan, President",
  "text": "It is clearly the domestic main cabin, especially leisure travelers booking close in. Premium and loyalty are holding up, and corporate is flat rather than down. International long-haul is fine."
 },
 {
  "seq": 18,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "Next question, Bartholomew Nkemelu with Steadman Rowe."
 },
 {
  "seq": 19,
  "speaker": "Bartholomew Nkemelu, Steadman Rowe",
  "text": "Two quick ones on fuel. Are you hedged at all for the summer, and are you still committed to your sustainable aviation fuel targets given the cost pressure?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 20,
  "speaker": "Ruairi Tanaka-Holm, CFO",
  "text": "We do not hedge fuel; we have found over time that the cost of hedging outweighs the benefit, and lower jet fuel prices are actually one of the few tailwinds this year. On sustainable aviation fuel, we still plan for it to be about 1% of our consumption by 2027. We are buying what is available at sensible prices, and we will not let that program become a material cost headwind."
 },
 {
  "seq": 21,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "Our next question comes from Ilse Vandermeer with Corbin & Hale."
 },
 {
  "seq": 22,
  "speaker": "Ilse Vandermeer, Corbin & Hale",
  "text": "Can you update us on the engine inspections on your newest narrowbodies? How many aircraft are parked, and is that in the June-quarter guide? And separately, where are you on the flight-attendant contract?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 23,
  "speaker": "Folasade Brennan, President",
  "text": "Nine aircraft are parked awaiting engine inspections, down from fourteen in January, and compensation from the engine manufacturer covers most of the cost. It is fully reflected in the June-quarter guide. On flight attendants, talks with the union are constructive, we have tentative agreements on scheduling and reserve rules, and we are not planning for any disruption this summer."
 },
 {
  "seq": 24,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "I would add that our people are the reason we led the industry in reliability this quarter, and we intend to reach a fair contract that reflects that. We have also kept our profit-sharing program intact, and we expect to continue paying it out this year."
 },
 {
  "seq": 25,
  "speaker": "Operator",
  "text": "We will now take questions from the media. Our first question is from Rosalind Ebbesen with the Midland Courier."
 },
 {
  "seq": 26,
  "speaker": "Rosalind Ebbesen, Midland Courier",
  "text": "Will the Raleigh expansion go ahead, and will any of the capacity cuts mean job losses?"
 },
 {
  "seq": 27,
  "speaker": "Ewan Castellano, CEO",
  "text": "Raleigh goes ahead as planned. There are no furloughs or layoffs planned; we will manage staffing through attrition and slower hiring. That concludes our call. Thank you all."
 }
]
